From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fogtape <lilixu3210@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 10:40:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] src: fix QUIC receive time after session creation

A server Initial packet creates an ngtcp2 connection after Endpoint::Receive
caches its clock. Passing that earlier timestamp to the first read violates
ngtcp2 monotonicity and aborts the process. Resolve read time inside
Session::ReadPacket, after connection creation, while retaining receive
batching and deferred writes.

Assisted-by: Codex
---
 src/quic/endpoint.cc | 8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/quic/endpoint.cc b/src/quic/endpoint.cc
index 13ca955fa2..e12efd5f51 100644
--- a/src/quic/endpoint.cc
+++ b/src/quic/endpoint.cc
@@ -1392,13 +1392,15 @@ void Endpoint::Receive(const uint8_t* data,
     // received in the same I/O burst are processed before any responses
     // are generated. The deferred flush via BindingData's uv_check
     // callback calls SendPendingData once per dirty session after all
-    // packets in the burst have been read.
+    // packets in the burst have been read. Let ReadPacket sample its own
+    // timestamp: accepting an Initial packet constructs a session after the
+    // endpoint's cached `now`, and ngtcp2 requires read timestamps to be at
+    // least settings.initial_ts. Reusing `now` aborts the first server read.
     if (session->ReadPacket(pkt_data,
                             pkt_len,
                             local_address,
                             remote_address,
-                            PacketInfo(),
-                            now)) {
+                            PacketInfo())) {
       STAT_INCREMENT_N(Stats, bytes_received, pkt_len);
       STAT_INCREMENT(Stats, packets_received);
     }
